> If you can devise another means of ensuring a level playing field with 
> client-server competitive Nethack, I'd be very interested to hear it.

Allowing for some degree of gratuitous hyperbole on my part, the
options are all reducible to:

1) Any client goes, and it's down to who can write (or download) the best
   'bot. (This is the level playing field you're proposing.)

2) Gentleman's (Gentlepersons... Gentlebeings) agreement. When I set
   up my server, I say "please use clients which have property X but
   which do not have property Y". You do so, because doing otherwise
   is simply unsportsm... er, simply not cricket. And, if you don't
   like those rules, there are plenty of other servers with different
   rules, or you can run your own. (This is my own preferred solution.)

3) Pretend to programmatically restrict things to a pre-approved set
   of clients. This is actually just doing (1), but with a false sense
   of security and the addition of both hassle and bragging rights for
   those who want to cheat.

   Stuff like PunkBuster on the various PC shoot-'em-ups, and also
   stuff like closed platforms and so-called "trusted computing"
   are in this category.

4) Physical security: Make players use software you provide on hardware
   you control. This one actually can be secure if done right, but
   who's going to bother for a video game? (Maybe if there were 
   nethack tournaments with cash prizes. The Omegathon needs a
   nethack segment...)

